1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,833
Please use Walker to convert my game
design document about a

2
00:00:03,133 --> 00:00:06,267
coding-agent platformer into one small
revision: put Clawd in

3
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:08,200
the existing level. Preserve the
mechanics.

4
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:10,300
I'm Liam, in for Professor Bear.

5
00:00:10,767 --> 00:00:12,967
His project is Walker Jumpman Clawd.

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,333
The opening prompt is a suggested handoff,
not a saved conversation.

7
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:20,600
We have changed the character, not
completed the assignment.

8
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:23,467
Clawd now moves through the existing First
Steps level.

9
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:25,567
The physics and layout remain the
starter's.

10
00:00:26,067 --> 00:00:28,100
A separate gallery contains eighteen
animations.

11
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:31,933
Task mini-goals, new hazards, and the
required level extension

12
00:00:31,933 --> 00:00:35,567
are later decisions. This is the first
revision of a semester-long

13
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,100
example.

14
00:00:37,133 --> 00:00:38,967
Predict what might break. Build one
change.

15
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,067
Play it, inspect the evidence, revise,
then record the version.

16
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:45,267
Here the prediction is specific: Clawd's
wide arms might make

17
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:47,100
the unchanged collision box misleading.

18
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,133
A small experiment gives us a question to
answer.

19
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:53,100
Changing the character, level, and physics
together would make

20
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:54,467
that answer harder to isolate.

21
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:57,133
Here is the actual Godot level.

22
00:00:57,467 --> 00:01:00,367
Enter starts, left and right move, and the
character stops after

23
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:03,567
release. Clawd's legs now animate with
movement; the ground

24
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:05,267
and controls have not been redesigned.

25
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:08,433
These are scripted normal-input captures,
not Professor Bear's

26
00:01:08,433 --> 00:01:11,433
playtest. The first visual question is
simple: can you read

27
00:01:11,433 --> 00:01:12,767
this character at game size?

28
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:15,667
Watch the feet leave the ground and
return.

29
00:01:16,133 --> 00:01:18,900
Holding Space does not bounce again on
landing, and another

30
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,133
press in the air does not create a double
jump.

31
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:24,600
The airborne drawing holds a pose while
physics supplies the

32
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:28,367
trajectory. An animation named jump must
not secretly add another

33
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:29,200
movement mechanic.

34
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,167
This enlarged view uses the actual Godot
drawing routine.

35
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:36,467
The blue rectangle marks the unchanged
eighteen by twenty-eight

36
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,367
collision reference. Clawd's arms extend
beyond it.

37
00:01:39,633 --> 00:01:42,933
That is a real first-pass tradeoff, not a
solved design problem.

38
00:01:43,433 --> 00:01:46,267
Bear should test whether the mismatch
feels forgiving or confusing

39
00:01:46,267 --> 00:01:48,167
before deciding on a second change.

40
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:52,167
Into the spikes: failure, a brief error
animation, one retry,

41
00:01:52,167 --> 00:01:53,867
then control returns at the start.

42
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:56,067
We did not invent a new hazard system.

43
00:01:56,567 --> 00:01:59,400
The character's reaction is presentation,
while the session

44
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,633
still owns the reset. Watch the result
before deciding whether

45
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:04,567
that visual reaction explains enough.

46
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:08,033
Missing the gap still ends the attempt and
starts another.

47
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:10,733
This is not the same test as touching
spikes.

48
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:14,167
Both need checking after a visual change
because a new silhouette

49
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,200
can change what a landing appears to mean.

50
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:19,467
The observed retry works; whether the
failure reads clearly

51
00:02:19,467 --> 00:02:20,800
is still a human judgment.

52
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,667
Here the jump input arrives just after
leaving a ledge, and

53
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:28,067
the character still rises. This is the
starter's coyote window,

54
00:02:28,267 --> 00:02:29,367
not a new Clawd power.

55
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:32,133
The recording demonstrates this particular
late jump.

56
00:02:32,567 --> 00:02:35,467
Boundary tests separately check the
accepted and expired tick

57
00:02:35,467 --> 00:02:38,933
ages; one attractive replay does not prove
every timing case.

58
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:43,167
Now Space arrives before landing, and the
buffered request produces

59
00:02:43,167 --> 00:02:46,167
the next jump. The controller consumes
that request once.

60
00:02:46,567 --> 00:02:48,300
This is different from holding a key
forever.

61
00:02:48,767 --> 00:02:51,700
Keeping the original input behavior gives
us a stable baseline

62
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:53,200
while we inspect the new character.

63
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:56,067
The art is the experiment; the jump rules
are not.

64
00:02:57,133 --> 00:03:00,233
Escape pauses the jump, Enter resumes, and
R restarts.

65
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:03,600
The capture also sends a labelled
simulated focus-loss event,

66
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:05,500
returns to the menu, and starts again.

67
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,067
Pause freezes the animation clock as well
as the player's motion.

68
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,500
These scripted checks demonstrate the
recorded controls, not

69
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:14,467
an unstated human usability test.

70
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,367
The menu's button also resumes and starts
the game with mouse

71
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:21,800
input. It is easy to check only the
keyboard path you usually

72
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,433
use. A small revision deserves a small but
deliberate regression

73
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:28,433
pass: does the rest of the existing
interface still do what

74
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:29,067
it did before?

75
00:03:30,467 --> 00:03:33,333
Clawd clears the original steps and gaps,
reaches the same finish,

76
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:35,733
and celebrates. Enter starts a fresh run.

77
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,200
This recorded route completes without a
death.

78
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:41,667
It is not yet the proposed agentic-loop
game: the flag is still

79
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:44,700
the original flag. Keeping that
distinction visible is part

80
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:46,333
of explaining what we actually built.

81
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:48,700
A first scene can appear quickly.

82
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:50,700
Refinement is where the work continues.

83
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,600
Make an improvement today; come back for
another improvement

84
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:54,900
or test in the next session.

85
00:03:55,433 --> 00:03:57,900
If an attempt does not work, record what
you tried, observed,

86
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:01,167
and learned in Frictional. Do not
manufacture struggle or pretend

87
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:03,300
these changes happened across several
days.

88
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:06,267
This iteration's entries are from one
actual session.

89
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:10,500
Bear's longer-term idea is a coding agent
crossing mini-goals:

90
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:13,567
a task completed, another task checked,
and eventually a full

91
00:04:13,567 --> 00:04:16,867
agentic loop finished. Those are design
notes, not features

92
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:20,167
in today's build. The level extension is
still required for

93
00:04:20,167 --> 00:04:23,300
Assignment One. Starting with Clawd's
appearance lets Bear decide

94
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:25,533
the next step from something he can
actually play.

95
00:04:26,467 --> 00:04:28,733
Idle, bounce, wave, look, walk, and run.

96
00:04:29,067 --> 00:04:31,233
Watch the four legs alternate and the eyes
scan.

97
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,800
These are actual Godot gallery previews of
the ported library.

98
00:04:35,233 --> 00:04:38,233
Bounce and wave are available artwork, not
player commands in

99
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:41,333
this level. The gallery lets us judge
options before choosing

100
00:04:41,333 --> 00:04:42,133
more behavior.

101
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,300
Think, type, sleep, error, nod, and shake.

102
00:04:45,767 --> 00:04:48,633
Their names suggest possible future
coding-agent moments, but

103
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:51,267
naming an animation does not implement a
task system.

104
00:04:51,833 --> 00:04:54,667
Error currently reacts to failure; the
others remain catalogue

105
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:58,267
options. Choose a future state for a
reason, then test whether

106
00:04:58,267 --> 00:04:59,833
the animation communicates it.

107
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:03,267
Dance, stretch, crouch, jump, spin, and
celebrate.

108
00:05:03,567 --> 00:05:06,700
Spin flips horizontally instead of
rotating the pixel rectangles.

109
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:10,000
The gallery jump is decorative; gameplay
uses its held pose

110
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:11,433
while physics moves the body.

111
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,767
Celebrate is connected to the current
finish.

112
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:16,767
All eighteen are here to inspect, not a
promise of eighteen

113
00:05:16,767 --> 00:05:17,200
powers.

114
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:21,600
The verdict: one recognizable character
change, preserved mechanics,

115
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:23,367
and a visible question about collision.

116
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:26,867
The A I wrote and tested the
implementation; Bear supplied the

117
00:05:26,867 --> 00:05:29,233
intent and chooses what deserves another
iteration.

118
00:05:29,733 --> 00:05:31,667
Start early enough to inspect the result.

119
00:05:32,133 --> 00:05:35,267
A test that reveals a problem can move the
project forward without

120
00:05:35,267 --> 00:05:36,433
making the feature complete.

121
00:05:37,467 --> 00:05:39,933
Your turn. Please inspect Walker Jumpman
Clawd.

122
00:05:39,933 --> 00:05:42,067
Propose one visual improvement and one
test.

123
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:44,733
Preserve the mechanics and log the result
in Frictional.

124
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:47,833
Before accepting the change, check that it
answers a specific

125
00:05:47,833 --> 00:05:50,833
observation, leaves the existing controls
intact, and names

126
00:05:50,833 --> 00:05:52,333
what the test does not establish.

127
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:53,800
Then let a human play.

128
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:55,567
Liam, in for Professor Bear.
